[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Tutorial: Docker Integration, Docker Pipeline Plugin, and Container Agents in Jenkins"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Modern CI/CD pipelines require isolated, reproducible, and scalable build environments. Jenkins achieves this through deep Docker integration and the Docker Pipeline Plugin, allowing teams to run builds inside ephemeral container agents. This tutorial walks through setting up Docker integration, leveraging the Docker Pipeline plugin, and configuring dynamic container agents."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"Prerequisites"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"A running Jenkins controller with administrative privileges."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Docker installed on the host machine running Jenkins."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The Docker Pipeline plugin installed via Jenkins Plugin Manager (Manage Jenkins > Plugins)."},{"order":7,"type":"heading","text":"Step 1: Configuring Docker Daemon Access"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"For Jenkins to spin up containers or communicate with the Docker daemon, the Jenkins user on the host machine must have permission to access the Docker socket (/var/run/docker.sock). Run the following commands on your host server:"},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"sudo usermod -aG docker jenkins
sudo systemctl restart jenkins"},{"order":10,"type":"heading","text":"Step 2: Defining a Basic Docker Pipeline"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Docker Pipeline plugin extends Jenkins Declarative Pipeline syntax, enabling you to run entire stages or specific steps inside a designated Docker container. Create a new Pipeline job in Jenkins and use the following Jenkinsfile snippet:"},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"pipeline {
    agent none
    stages {
        stage('Build with Maven') {
            agent {
                docker {
                    image 'maven:3.8.6-openjdk-11'
                    args '-v /root/.m2:/root/.m2'
                }
            }
            steps {
                sh 'mvn --version'
                sh 'mvn clean package'
            }
        }
    }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Breaking Down the Pipeline Syntax"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"agent none: Disables a global agent, requiring each stage to declare its own execution environment."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"docker.image: Pulls and spins up the specified container image (maven:3.8.6-openjdk-11) for the duration of the stage."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"args: Passes optional flags to the Docker run command. Here, we mount the host Maven repository directory to persist dependencies across builds."},{"order":17,"type":"heading","text":"Step 3: Building and Pushing Custom Images within a Pipeline"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can also script the Docker CLI directly inside your pipeline script using the docker object provided by the plugin. This is ideal for building application images and pushing them to a registry."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"pipeline {
    agent any
    environment {
        REGISTRY_CREDENTIALS = 'my-docker-hub-credentials'
        IMAGE_NAME = 'myorganization/myapp'
    }
    stages {
        stage('Checkout') {
            steps {
                checkout scm
            }
        }
        stage('Build Image') {
            steps {
                script {
                    app = docker.build("${IMAGE_NAME}:${env.BUILD_NUMBER}")
                }
            }
        }
        stage('Push Image') {
            steps {
                script {
                    docker.withRegistry('https://index.docker.io/v1/', "${REGISTRY_CREDENTIALS}") {
                        app.push()
                        app.push('latest')
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
}"},{"order":20,"type":"heading","text":"Step 4: Utilizing Container Agents for Scalability"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"Instead of running containers transiently inside a stage, you can define a container agent at the top level of your pipeline. This provisions a long-lived container for the entire workspace execution, optimizing resource usage and keeping steps clean."},{"order":22,"type":"code","text":"pipeline {
    agent {
        docker {
            image 'node:18-alpine'
            args '-p 3000:3000'
        }
    }
    stages {
        stage('Install Dependencies') {
            steps {
                sh 'npm install'
            }
        },
        stage('Run Tests') {
            steps {
                sh 'npm test'
            }
        }
    }
}"},{"order":23,"type":"heading","text":"Best Practices and Troubleshooting"},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cleanup: Always clean up dangling Docker images and containers on the Jenkins controller to prevent disk space exhaustion."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Docker-in-Docker (DinD): If your Jenkins controller itself runs inside a container, avoid mounting the host socket directly; instead, use proper DinD sidecar configurations."},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Permissions: If you encounter permission denied errors on /var/run/docker.sock, verify the Jenkins user group memberships and restart the service."}]
